        "correct_answer": "D",
        "explanation": "An Epworth Sleepiness Scale (ESS) score of 18 indicates severe excessive daytime sleepiness requiring urgent sleep study.\n\n**Epworth Sleepiness Scale (ESS):**\n\n**Description:**\n- Self-administered 8-item questionnaire\n- Measures likelihood of falling asleep in 8 common situations\n- Each item scored 0-3 (0=never doze, 3=high chance)\n- Total score: 0-24\n\n**Eight Situations Assessed:**\n1. Sitting and reading\n2. Watching television\n3. Sitting inactive in public place\n4. Passenger in car for 1 hour\n5. Lying down to rest in afternoon\n6. Sitting and talking to someone\n7. Sitting quietly after lunch (no alcohol)\n8. In car while stopped in traffic\n\n**Score Interpretation:**\n- **0-7**: Normal - adequate sleep, no excessive daytime sleepiness\n- **8-9**: Borderline - may warrant further assessment\n- **10-15**: Moderate excessive daytime sleepiness - sleep study recommended\n- **16-24**: Severe excessive daytime sleepiness - urgent evaluation required\n\n**Clinical Significance of ESS 18:**\n- Falls into severe category (>16)\n- High risk of:\n  - Motor vehicle accidents (falls asleep during meetings → likely during driving)\n  - Workplace incidents\n  - Underlying sleep disorder (OSA most common)\n- Warrants urgent investigation with polysomnography\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (Normal)**: Score >10 is abnormal; 18 is severe\n- **B (Mild/lifestyle only)**: 18 is severe, not mild; requires diagnostic workup\n- **C (Moderate)**: 18 exceeds moderate threshold (10-15); this is severe\n- **E (Invalid)**: No indication score is invalid; 18 is within possible range (0-24)\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- ESS widely used in sleep clinics across Australia\n- High ESS score (>10) supports MBS eligibility for sleep studies\n- Commercial drivers: ESS >16 may require fitness-to-drive assessment\n- Shift work can exacerbate underlying sleep disorders\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** ESS is a screening tool, not diagnostic. High ESS indicates need for objective testing (polysomnography) to diagnose underlying cause (OSA, narcolepsy, insufficient sleep syndrome). This patient's shift work complicates assessment but doesn't change need for urgent evaluation given severe score.",
