        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "This patient has central sleep apnoea (CSA), characterised by absent respiratory effort during apnoeic episodes.\n\n**Central vs Obstructive Sleep Apnoea:**\n\n**Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (OSA):**\n- Upper airway collapse\n- **Respiratory effort present** (paradoxical chest/abdominal movement)\n- Airflow ceases despite continued breathing attempts\n- Obesity common\n- Snoring, gasping\n\n**Central Sleep Apnoea (CSA):**\n- **Absent respiratory effort** (no chest/abdominal movement)\n- Brainstem fails to signal respiratory muscles\n- Airflow ceases because brain stops trying to breathe\n- Associated with:\n  - Heart failure (40-50% of severe HF patients)\n  - Stroke\n  - Opioid use\n  - High altitude\n- Cheyne-Stokes respiration pattern common\n\n**Cheyne-Stokes Breathing:**\n- Crescendo-decrescendo respiratory pattern\n- Cyclic apnoeas alternating with hyperpnoea\n- Pathognomonic for heart failure-related CSA\n- Due to:\n  - Prolonged circulation time\n  - Altered chemoreceptor sensitivity\n  - Increased ventilatory response to CO₂\n\n**Clinical Clues in This Case:**\n1. **Heart failure (LVEF 30%)** - strongest risk factor for CSA\n2. **Absent respiratory effort** - distinguishes CSA from OSA\n3. **Cheyne-Stokes pattern** - classic for cardiac CSA\n4. **Moderate-severe AHI (28)** - significant sleep-disordered breathing\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (OSA)**: Would show respiratory effort during apnoeas; snoring/gasping typical\n- **C (Mixed apnoea)**: Has both obstructive and central events; question specifies absent effort throughout\n- **D (Insomnia)**: Sleep initiation/maintenance problem; no apnoeas or AHI elevation\n- **E (PLMD)**: Limb movements, not apnoeas; different PSG findings\n\n**Management of CSA in Heart Failure:**\n1. **Optimize heart failure therapy** (ACE-I, beta-blocker, diuretics, CRT if indicated)\n2. **Adaptive servo-ventilation (ASV)** - CONTRAINDICATED in HF with LVEF <45% (SERVE-HF trial showed increased mortality)\n3. **BiPAP-ST (spontaneous-timed)** - alternative positive airway pressure mode\n4. **Oxygen therapy** - may reduce central events\n5. **Consider cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT)** - improves CSA in some patients\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- CSA in heart failure associated with worse prognosis\n- ASV NOT funded on PBS for HF patients (due to SERVE-HF findings)\n- Cardiologist + sleep physician co-management recommended\n\n**Critical Warning:** CPAP/ASV can be harmful in advanced heart failure with CSA. Focus on optimizing cardiac therapy first.",
