        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "This patient has nocturnal hypoventilation due to neuromuscular disease requiring nocturnal BiPAP.\n\n**Nocturnal Hypoventilation in Neuromuscular Disease:**\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\n- Respiratory muscle weakness (diaphragm, intercostals)\n- Reduced tidal volumes during sleep (especially REM sleep)\n- Inadequate alveolar ventilation → CO₂ retention and hypoxaemia\n- REM sleep most affected (skeletal muscle atonia except diaphragm)\n\n**Clinical Features:**\n- **Morning headaches** (CO₂ retention)\n- **Daytime somnolence** (fragmented sleep, hypoxaemia)\n- **Cor pulmonale** (ankle oedema, right heart failure)\n- Normal daytime oxygen but nocturnal desaturations\n- Orthopnoea (diaphragmatic weakness)\n- Recurrent chest infections\n\n**Diagnostic Tests:**\n- **Overnight oximetry**: Shows isolated nocturnal desaturations\n- **Overnight capnography**: Elevated PtcCO₂ (transcutaneous CO₂)\n- **Spirometry**: FVC <50% predicted indicates risk\n- **Supine FVC**: >25% drop from sitting suggests diaphragm weakness\n- **ABG**: May show chronic compensated respiratory acidosis\n\n**Why BiPAP is Correct:**\n\nBiPAP (bilevel PAP) provides:\n- **IPAP (inspiratory PAP)**: Higher pressure during inspiration → augments tidal volume\n- **EPAP (expiratory PAP)**: Lower pressure during expiration → prevents atelectasis\n- **Ventilatory support**: Offloads respiratory muscles\n- **Improves gas exchange**: Reduces CO₂, increases O₂\n- **Preserves sleep quality**: Reduces arousals from hypoxaemia/hypercapnia\n\n**BiPAP vs CPAP:**\n- CPAP provides single pressure (pneumatic splint for OSA)\n- BiPAP provides two pressures (ventilatory support)\n- Neuromuscular hypoventilation requires ventilatory support, not just airway splinting\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (LTOT)**: Oxygen alone dangerous - suppresses hypoxic drive, worsens CO₂ retention; doesn't address hypoventilation\n- **C (CPAP)**: Single pressure; doesn't provide ventilatory support; inadequate for hypoventilation\n- **D (Tracheostomy)**: Too invasive as initial therapy; reserved for severe bulbar dysfunction or NIV failure\n- **E (Daytime NIV only)**: Nocturnal hypoventilation occurs during sleep; needs nocturnal support\n\n**Australian Guidelines (TSANZ):**\n- NIV indicated when:\n  - Nocturnal desaturations (<88% for >5 minutes)\n  - PaCO₂ >45 mmHg\n  - FVC <50% predicted in neuromuscular disease\n- BiPAP-ST (spontaneous-timed) mode often used\n- MBS funding available (Item 11912 - NIV for chronic respiratory failure)\n\n**Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy Specific:**\n- Progressive neuromuscular disorder\n- Respiratory failure leading cause of death\n- NIV prolongs survival (median 5-10 years)\n- Start NIV when symptomatic or FVC <50%\n- May eventually need 24-hour ventilation\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** NEVER give supplemental oxygen alone for hypoventilation - it may worsen CO₂ retention. Always provide ventilatory support (BiPAP) to improve minute ventilation.",
