        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "Spirometry with bronchodilator reversibility is the most appropriate next investigation for persistent chronic cough after initial empirical treatment.\n\n**Chronic Cough Definition:**\n- Cough lasting **>8 weeks** (this patient: 12 weeks)\n- Acute: <3 weeks\n- Subacute: 3-8 weeks\n- Chronic: >8 weeks\n\n**Common Causes of Chronic Cough (Australian Data):**\n\n**\"Big 3\" Causes (account for 90-95%):**\n1. **Postnasal drip syndrome** (upper airway cough syndrome) - 20-40%\n2. **Asthma / eosinophilic bronchitis** - 25-30%\n3. **Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD)** - 10-40%\n\n**Other Causes:**\n- ACE inhibitor use (5-35% of users)\n- Bronchiectasis\n- Interstitial lung disease\n- Lung cancer (especially age >40, smokers)\n- Pertussis / post-infectious\n- Psychogenic / habit cough\n\n**Stepwise Investigation Approach:**\n\n**Step 1: History and Examination**\n- Exclude red flags (haemoptysis, weight loss, night sweats)\n- Medication review (ACE inhibitor?)\n- Smoking history\n- Chest X-ray (all patients with chronic cough)\n\n**Step 2: Empirical Treatment Trials** (already done in this case)\n- PPI for GORD (8-12 weeks)\n- Intranasal corticosteroids for postnasal drip (2-4 weeks)\n- If response → diagnosis confirmed\n- If no response → proceed to Step 3\n\n**Step 3: Objective Testing**\n- **Spirometry ± bronchodilator** (assess for asthma, obstruction)\n- If spirometry normal:\n  - Bronchial provocation test (methacholine/mannitol) for asthma\n  - Induced sputum eosinophils (eosinophilic bronchitis)\n- If still normal:\n  - Consider HRCT (ILD, bronchiectasis)\n  - Consider bronchoscopy (endobronchial lesion)\n\n**Why Spirometry is Next:**\n- **Non-invasive, safe, widely available**\n- **Asthma** is a leading cause of chronic cough (may be only symptom - \"cough-variant asthma\")\n- Patient denies wheeze, but asthma can present with cough alone\n- Normal spirometry doesn't exclude asthma (need provocation test if spirometry normal)\n- **Cost-effective**: Australian MBS Item 11506\n\n**Why Other Options Are Premature:**\n- **A (HRCT)**: More expensive, radiation exposure; reserve for when spirometry/provocation tests normal or if specific indication (ILD, bronchiectasis)\n- **C (Bronchoscopy)**: Invasive; indicated if:\n  - Haemoptysis\n  - Abnormal CXR/CT\n  - Localized wheeze\n  - Smoker age >40 with no cause found\n  - Not first-line for simple chronic cough\n- **D (Sputum culture)**: Indicated if productive cough, suspicion of TB or atypical infection; this patient has dry cough\n- **E (Methacholine challenge)**: Appropriate IF spirometry is normal; do simpler test first\n\n**Australian Guidelines (TSANZ Chronic Cough Algorithm 2015):**\n1. History, exam, CXR\n2. Treat empirically (GORD, postnasal drip)\n3. If no response: Spirometry\n4. If normal spirometry: Bronchial provocation test\n5. If negative: HRCT, bronchoscopy\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** Up to 25% of chronic cough patients have multiple causes. If partial response to treatment, continue that treatment and investigate for additional causes.",
