        "correct_answer": "D",
        "explanation": "Bronchoscopy with biopsy is the most appropriate investigation for suspected lung cancer with an endobronchial lesion visible on CXR.\n\n**Haemoptysis Evaluation:**\n\n**Definition:**\n- Coughing up blood from respiratory tract\n- **Massive haemoptysis**: >200-600 mL per 24 hours (life-threatening)\n- **Non-massive**: Small-volume (this patient)\n\n**Common Causes by Age/Context:**\n\n**Smoker with Weight Loss + Hilar Mass:**\n- **Lung cancer** (most likely) - 20-30% of all haemoptysis\n- Squamous cell carcinoma (most likely to cause haemoptysis)\n\n**Other Causes:**\n- Bronchiectasis (most common cause overall in Australia)\n- Tuberculosis (especially recent migrants, immunosuppressed)\n- Pulmonary embolism\n- Pneumonia\n- Bronchitis\n- Vasculitis (Granulomatosis with polyangiitis)\n- AV malformation\n\n**Red Flags for Lung Cancer:**\n1. ✅ Age >40 years\n2. ✅ Smoker (>20 pack-years)\n3. ✅ Persistent haemoptysis (>3 weeks)\n4. ✅ Weight loss\n5. ✅ Abnormal CXR (hilar mass)\n\n**Investigation Algorithm:**\n\n**All Patients with Haemoptysis:**\n- Chest X-ray (first-line)\n- FBC (anaemia, thrombocytopaenia)\n- Coagulation studies\n\n**If CXR Abnormal or High-Risk Features:**\n- **CT chest with IV contrast** (staging, characterize mass)\n- **Bronchoscopy** (visualize airways, obtain tissue)\n\n**Bronchoscopy Indications:**\n- Age >40 with risk factors (smoking) and haemoptysis\n- Abnormal CXR (mass, infiltrate, atelectasis)\n- Massive haemoptysis (identify bleeding site)\n- Recurrent/persistent haemoptysis despite normal CXR in smokers\n\n**Why Bronchoscopy is Correct:**\n- **Diagnostic**: Hilar mass likely endobronchial (central lung cancer)\n- **Direct visualization**: Can see tumour, bleeding source\n- **Tissue diagnosis**: Biopsy for histology (essential for treatment planning)\n- **High yield**: 50-90% diagnostic rate for central tumours\n- **Guides treatment**: Histological subtype determines therapy (surgery, chemo, immunotherapy)\n\n**CT Chest First?**\n- Many clinicians do CT before bronchoscopy to:\n  - Characterize mass (size, location, mediastinal nodes)\n  - Assess resectability\n  - Guide bronchoscopy (know anatomy)\n- Both tests are complementary\n- However, bronchoscopy provides definitive tissue diagnosis\n\n**Why Other Options Are Less Appropriate:**\n- **A (Sputum cytology)**: Low sensitivity (30-60%); multiple samples needed; doesn't replace bronchoscopy; may be used if patient unfit for bronchoscopy\n- **B (CTPA)**: For suspected pulmonary embolism; not indicated here (mass on CXR, no dyspnoea/chest pain/leg swelling)\n- **C (CT chest)**: Important for staging but doesn't provide tissue diagnosis; bronchoscopy needed regardless\n- **E (PET-CT)**: For staging confirmed lung cancer; need histological diagnosis first\n\n**Correct Sequence:**\n1. **CXR** (done - shows hilar mass)\n2. **CT chest with contrast** (often done before bronchoscopy for planning)\n3. **Bronchoscopy with biopsy** (obtain tissue diagnosis)\n4. **PET-CT** (stage confirmed cancer)\n5. **MDT discussion** (plan treatment - surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy)\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- Two-week wait target for suspected lung cancer (Queensland, Victoria)\n- MBS Item 41889 (bronchoscopy with biopsy)\n- Rapid access lung cancer clinics in major centres\n- EBUS-TBNA (endobronchial ultrasound) increasingly used for mediastinal staging\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** In a smoker with haemoptysis and abnormal CXR, lung cancer must be excluded. Both CT and bronchoscopy are needed, but bronchoscopy provides tissue diagnosis which is essential before starting treatment.",
        "topic": "Respiratory - Haemoptysis Workup",
