        "id": "WEEK3_RESP_196",
        "question": "A 35-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis presents with progressive dyspnoea on exertion over 12 months. Examination reveals a loud P2 heart sound and elevated JVP. Transthoracic echocardiography shows estimated RVSP of 65 mmHg with normal left heart function. Right heart catheterisation confirms mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) of 32 mmHg with PAWP of 10 mmHg. What WHO functional classification best describes this pulmonary hypertension?",
        "options": {
            "A": "WHO Group 1 - Pulmonary arterial hypertension",
            "B": "WHO Group 2 - Due to left heart disease",
            "C": "WHO Group 3 - Due to lung disease/hypoxia",
            "D": "WHO Group 4 - Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension",
            "E": "WHO Group 5 - Multifactorial/unclear mechanisms"
        },
        "correct_answer": "A",
        "explanation": "This patient has WHO Group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) associated with systemic sclerosis.\n\n**Pulmonary Hypertension Definition (2022 ESC/ERS):**\n- Mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) **≥20 mmHg** at rest (previous definition: ≥25)\n- Measured by right heart catheterisation (gold standard)\n\n**WHO Classification of Pulmonary Hypertension:**\n\n**Group 1: Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH)**\n- Pre-capillary PH: mPAP ≥20, PAWP ≤15, PVR >2 WU\n- **Causes:**\n  - Idiopathic (IPAH)\n  - Heritable (BMPR2, others)\n  - **Connective tissue disease (CTD-PAH)** ← THIS PATIENT\n    - Systemic sclerosis (10-15% develop PAH)\n    - SLE, MCTD, rheumatoid arthritis\n  - Congenital heart disease\n  - Portal hypertension\n  - Drugs (methamphetamines, dasatinib)\n  - HIV, schistosomiasis\n\n**Group 2: Left Heart Disease**\n- Post-capillary PH: mPAP ≥20, PAWP **>15**\n- Causes: LV systolic/diastolic dysfunction, valvular disease\n- Most common cause of PH overall\n\n**Group 3: Lung Disease/Hypoxia**\n- COPD, ILD, sleep-disordered breathing, chronic hypoxia\n- Usually mild PH (mPAP 20-35)\n\n**Group 4: Chronic Thromboembolic PH (CTEPH)**\n- Organized thrombi in pulmonary arteries\n- History of acute PE (not always)\n- V/Q scan shows mismatched defects\n- **Potentially curable** with pulmonary endarterectomy\n\n**Group 5: Unclear/Multifactorial**\n- Haematological (sickle cell, myeloproliferative)\n- Systemic (sarcoidosis, vasculitis)\n- Metabolic (glycogen storage disease)\n- Other (chronic renal failure on dialysis)\n\n**This Patient - Group 1 PAH:**\n\n**Criteria Met:**\n1. ✅ **mPAP 32 mmHg** (>20) - diagnostic for PH\n2. ✅ **PAWP 10 mmHg** (≤15) - pre-capillary (rules out Group 2)\n3. ✅ **Systemic sclerosis** - known cause of PAH (CTD-PAH)\n4. ✅ **Normal left heart function** (echo) - not Group 2\n5. ✅ No lung disease/CTEPH mentioned\n\n**Systemic Sclerosis-Associated PAH:**\n- Occurs in 10-15% of systemic sclerosis patients\n- Limited cutaneous SSc > diffuse SSc\n- Screen annually with:\n  - Echo (RVSP, RV function)\n  - DLCO (reduced if PAH develops)\n  - NT-proBNP\n- Worse prognosis than IPAH if diagnosed late\n- **Early detection crucial** (better treatment response)\n\n**Why Other Groups Are Incorrect:**\n- **B (Group 2)**: PAWP 10 (not >15); normal LV function; pre-capillary PH\n- **C (Group 3)**: No mention of COPD, ILD, or hypoxia; systemic sclerosis causes Group 1, not Group 3\n- **D (Group 4)**: No history of PE; no V/Q scan findings suggesting CTEPH\n- **E (Group 5)**: Systemic sclerosis is well-established Group 1 cause, not unclear mechanism\n\n**Management of PAH (Group 1):**\n\n**General Measures:**\n- Avoid pregnancy (high mortality)\n- Immunizations (influenza, pneumococcal)\n- Supervised exercise rehabilitation\n- Oxygen if hypoxaemic\n- Diuretics for fluid overload\n- Anticoagulation (controversial - NOT routinely for CTD-PAH)\n\n**PAH-Specific Therapies:**\n1. **Endothelin receptor antagonists** (bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan)\n2. **PDE-5 inhibitors** (sildenafil, tadalafil)\n3. **Prostacyclins** (epoprostenol IV, iloprost inhaled, treprostinil)\n4. **Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator** (riociguat)\n5. **Combination therapy** often needed\n\n**Australian PBS Access:**\n- PAH therapies on PBS with authority prescription\n- Requires right heart catheterisation confirmation\n- Specialist (respiratory or cardiologist with PH expertise)\n- ONLY for WHO Group 1 (NOT Groups 2, 3, 5)\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** Systemic sclerosis patients need annual PH screening (echo, DLCO, NT-proBNP). Early detection and treatment of PAH improves outcomes. PAH-specific drugs are ONLY effective in Group 1 - harmful in Groups 2 and 3.",
