        "id": "WEEK3_RESP_197",
        "question": "A 62-year-old man with severe COPD (FEV1 35% predicted) presents with worsening dyspnoea, orthopnoea, and bilateral ankle swelling over 2 weeks. Examination shows elevated JVP (8 cm), hepatomegaly (3 cm), and pitting oedema to knees. Chest X-ray demonstrates hyperinflation and right heart enlargement. What is the MOST appropriate initial management?",
        "options": {
            "A": "Increase bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids",
            "B": "Intravenous furosemide and fluid restriction",
            "C": "Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT)",
            "D": "Oral prednisolone 30 mg daily for COPD exacerbation",
            "E": "Start ACE inhibitor for heart failure"
        },
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "This patient has acute right heart failure (cor pulmonale) secondary to severe COPD requiring diuretic therapy and fluid restriction.\n\n**Cor Pulmonale Definition:**\n- Right ventricular (RV) dysfunction resulting from **lung disease** or pulmonary vascular disease\n- Distinguished from RV failure due to left heart disease\n- COPD is most common cause (90%)\n\n**Pathophysiology in COPD:**\n\n**Mechanisms Leading to Cor Pulmonale:**\n1. **Chronic hypoxia** (pulmonary vasoconstriction)\n2. **Loss of pulmonary capillary bed** (emphysema)\n3. **Pulmonary hypertension** develops\n4. **RV pressure overload** → RV hypertrophy, then RV failure\n5. **Systemic venous congestion**\n\n**Clinical Features - Right Heart Failure:**\n\n**Symptoms:**\n- Worsening dyspnoea, orthopnoea\n- Abdominal distension (ascites)\n- Ankle swelling\n- Fatigue\n\n**Signs:**\n- ✅ **Elevated JVP** (most sensitive sign)\n- ✅ **Hepatomegaly** (may be pulsatile)\n- ✅ **Peripheral oedema** (ankles, legs, sacrum)\n- Loud P2 (pulmonary component second heart sound)\n- RV heave (left parasternal)\n- Hepatojugular reflux\n- Ascites (severe cases)\n- Pleural effusions\n\n**Investigations:**\n- **CXR**: Cardiomegaly (RV enlargement), prominent pulmonary arteries, signs of lung disease\n- **ECG**: Right axis deviation, P pulmonale (peaked P waves), RVH (R in V1, S in V5/V6)\n- **Echo**: RV dilatation/dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension (RVSP), exclude LV dysfunction\n- **ABG**: Hypoxaemia, may have hypercapnia\n- **BNP/NT-proBNP**: Elevated (not specific - elevated in both RV and LV failure)\n\n**Management of Acute Cor Pulmonale:**\n\n**Immediate Management:**\n1. **Oxygen therapy** (correct hypoxaemia - target SpO₂ 88-92% in COPD)\n2. **Diuretics** (furosemide IV) - reduce preload, hepatic congestion, oedema\n3. **Fluid restriction** (1-1.5L/day)\n4. **Treat underlying COPD exacerbation** if present:\n   - Bronchodilators (salbutamol, ipratropium)\n   - Corticosteroids (if exacerbation)\n   - Antibiotics (if infective)\n5. **Treat precipitants**: Infection, PE, arrhythmia\n\n**Long-Term Management:**\n1. **Treat underlying lung disease** (optimize COPD therapy)\n2. **Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT)** if:\n   - PaO₂ <55 mmHg (7.3 kPa) or\n   - PaO₂ 55-60 mmHg with polycythaemia/cor pulmonale\n   - Use ≥15 hours/day (proven survival benefit)\n3. **Diuretics** (oral furosemide, spironolactone)\n4. **Pulmonary rehabilitation**\n5. **Vaccination** (influenza, pneumococcal)\n6. **Consider lung transplant** if severe, progressive disease\n\n**Why Diuretics (Option B) Are Correct:**\n- Reduces RV preload\n- Relieves hepatic congestion (reduces hepatomegaly)\n- Reduces peripheral oedema\n- Improves dyspnoea\n- Immediate symptomatic benefit\n- Fluid restriction synergistic effect\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (Increase bronchodilators/ICS)**: Important for COPD management but doesn't address fluid overload; won't rapidly reduce oedema/JVP\n- **C (LTOT)**: Essential long-term therapy for cor pulmonale but doesn't acutely treat fluid overload; takes weeks-months to reduce pulmonary hypertension\n- **D (Oral prednisolone)**: Appropriate if COPD exacerbation present, but question describes heart failure symptoms, not acute bronchospasm/infection; steroids may worsen fluid retention\n- **E (ACE inhibitor)**: NO role in cor pulmonale; may worsen hypoxia-induced pulmonary vasoconstriction; used for LV failure, not RV failure\n\n**Critical Points:**\n\n**ACE Inhibitors/ARBs in Cor Pulmonale:**\n- **NOT recommended**\n- May worsen pulmonary hypertension\n- Risk of systemic hypotension\n- Unlike LV failure, RAAS blockade not beneficial in isolated RV failure\n\n**Digoxin in Cor Pulmonale:**\n- Limited role\n- May be used if atrial fibrillation present\n- Not first-line for isolated RV failure\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- LTOT funded by Australian government (Home Oxygen Service)\n- Requires ABG documentation of hypoxaemia\n- Pulmonary rehabilitation available through public hospitals\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** Cor pulmonale in COPD is managed by treating underlying lung disease + managing fluid overload. LTOT is the only intervention proven to improve survival in COPD with cor pulmonale (START study). Diuretics provide symptomatic relief but don't alter prognosis.",
        "topic": "Respiratory - Right Heart Failure (Cor Pulmonale)",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "australian_context": True,
