    {
        "id": "WEEK3_RESP_198",
        "question": "A 55-year-old man with end-stage COPD develops progressive right heart failure despite maximum medical therapy. His resting arterial blood gas on room air shows: pH 7.38, PaCO₂ 55 mmHg, PaO₂ 52 mmHg (6.9 kPa), HCO₃⁻ 30 mmol/L. What is the MOST appropriate next management step?",
        "options": {
            "A": "Increase oral furosemide dose",
            "B": "Start long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT)",
            "C": "Commence ACE inhibitor",
            "D": "Add spironolactone",
            "E": "Referral for lung transplant assessment"
        },
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "This patient meets criteria for long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT), which improves survival in COPD with chronic respiratory failure and cor pulmonale.\n\n**Long-Term Oxygen Therapy (LTOT) Indications:**\n\n**Absolute Indications (PBS/Government Funded in Australia):**\n\n**Criterion 1:**\n- **PaO₂ ≤55 mmHg (7.3 kPa)** at rest on room air\n- ✅ **This patient: PaO₂ 52 mmHg (6.9 kPa)** - MEETS CRITERIA\n\n**Criterion 2:**\n- PaO₂ 56-59 mmHg (7.4-7.8 kPa) PLUS evidence of:\n  - **Cor pulmonale** (right heart failure) OR\n  - **Pulmonary hypertension** (echo/RHC) OR\n  - **Polycythaemia** (Hct >55%)\n\n**Additional Requirements:**\n- **Optimized medical therapy** (maximum bronchodilators, cessation of smoking)\n- **Stable disease** (not during acute exacerbation - wait 4-6 weeks after exacerbation)\n- **Documented on two occasions** 3+ weeks apart\n- **Compliance expected** (patient willing/able to use ≥15 hours/day)\n\n**LTOT Usage Requirements:**\n- **≥15 hours per day** (including overnight)\n- 24 hours/day preferable if tolerated\n- More hours = better survival benefit\n\n**Evidence Base for LTOT:**\n\n**Landmark Trials:**\n\n**1. MRC (Medical Research Council) Trial (1981):**\n- LTOT 15+ hours/day vs no oxygen\n- Mortality reduction: 50% at 5 years\n- Improved survival, less polycythaemia\n\n**2. NOTT (Nocturnal Oxygen Therapy Trial) (1980):**\n- Continuous (24h) vs nocturnal (12h) oxygen\n- Continuous oxygen superior\n- Mortality reduction with longer duration\n\n**Mechanisms of Benefit:**\n1. **Reverses hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction** → reduces pulmonary hypertension\n2. **Reduces RV afterload** → improves RV function, reduces cor pulmonale progression\n3. **Improves tissue oxygen delivery**\n4. **Reduces polycythaemia** (secondary to chronic hypoxia)\n5. **Improves exercise tolerance and quality of life**\n6. **Reduces hospital admissions**\n\n**This Patient's ABG Analysis:**\n\n**Interpretation:**\n- **pH 7.38** (normal - compensated)\n- **PaCO₂ 55 mmHg** (elevated - chronic hypercapnia)\n- **PaO₂ 52 mmHg** (low - meets LTOT criteria)\n- **HCO₃⁻ 30 mmol/L** (elevated - metabolic compensation for respiratory acidosis)\n\n**Diagnosis:** Chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure (Type 2) with metabolic compensation\n\n**LTOT Target in COPD:**\n- **SpO₂ 88-92%** (not 94-98% like normal patients)\n- Higher oxygen may:\n  - Worsen hypercapnia (loss of hypoxic drive)\n  - Worsen V/Q matching\n  - Precipitate CO₂ narcosis\n- Titrate oxygen flow rate to achieve target saturation\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (Increase furosemide)**: Important for fluid management but doesn't address underlying chronic hypoxaemia; not disease-modifying\n- **C (ACE inhibitor)**: Contraindicated in isolated RV failure/cor pulmonale; no survival benefit; may worsen hypotension\n- **D (Add spironolactone)**: May help with diuresis but doesn't address hypoxaemia; LTOT is evidence-based survival intervention\n- **E (Lung transplant)**: May be appropriate if LTOT fails and patient meets criteria, but LTOT should be trialed first; less invasive, proven benefit\n\n**Australian LTOT Service:**\n\n**Prescription Requirements:**\n- Respiratory physician or specialist assessment\n- Two ABGs 3+ weeks apart (both meeting criteria)\n- Patient education (oxygen safety, flow rates)\n- Home assessment (safety, electricity access)\n\n**Equipment Provided:**\n- Oxygen concentrator (stationary)\n- Portable oxygen (cylinders or portable concentrator)\n- Backup oxygen supply\n- Pulse oximeter\n\n**PBS Funding:**\n- Government-funded through Home Oxygen Service\n- No out-of-pocket cost to patient\n- Ongoing equipment maintenance included\n\n**Safety Considerations:**\n- **No smoking** (fire/explosion risk - absolute contraindication)\n- Avoid naked flames, open fires\n- Electrical safety (concentrator)\n- Travel considerations (portable systems, notify airlines)\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** LTOT is the ONLY therapy proven to improve survival in COPD with chronic respiratory failure (along with smoking cessation). Must be used ≥15 hours/day to confer benefit. Higher flow rates or higher saturations are NOT better - target SpO₂ 88-92% to avoid CO₂ retention.",
