    {
        "id": "WEEK3_RESP_199",
        "question": "A 45-year-old woman presents with progressive dyspnoea on exertion. Spirometry results are as follows:\n\nFEV₁: 2.8 L (82% predicted)\nFVC: 3.0 L (70% predicted)\nFEV₁/FVC ratio: 0.93\nTLC: 3.8 L (65% predicted)\n\nWhat is the MOST likely pattern of lung disease?",
        "options": {
            "A": "Normal lung function",
            "B": "Obstructive lung disease",
            "C": "Restrictive lung disease",
            "D": "Mixed obstructive and restrictive disease",
            "E": "Small airways disease"
        },
        "correct_answer": "C",
        "explanation": "This patient has a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function tests.\n\n**Pulmonary Function Test (PFT) Interpretation:**\n\n**Key Measurements:**\n\n**Spirometry:**\n- **FEV₁** (Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second): Volume exhaled in first second of forced breath\n- **FVC** (Forced Vital Capacity): Total volume exhaled in forced breath\n- **FEV₁/FVC ratio**: Proportion of FVC exhaled in first second\n\n**Lung Volumes:**\n- **TLC** (Total Lung Capacity): Total volume of air in lungs at maximal inspiration\n- **RV** (Residual Volume): Air remaining after maximal expiration\n- **FRC** (Functional Residual Capacity): Air in lungs at end of normal expiration\n\n**Classification of Patterns:**\n\n**OBSTRUCTIVE Pattern:**\n- **FEV₁/FVC <0.70** (fixed ratio) OR <LLN (lower limit of normal)\n- FEV₁ reduced (severity: mild, moderate, severe, very severe)\n- FVC normal or reduced\n- TLC normal or **increased** (hyperinflation)\n- RV **increased** (air trapping)\n- Examples: COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis\n\n**RESTRICTIVE Pattern:**\n- **FEV₁/FVC ≥0.70** (normal or increased)\n- FEV₁ reduced (proportional to FVC)\n- FVC **reduced** (<80% predicted)\n- TLC **reduced** (<80% predicted) - DIAGNOSTIC\n- RV reduced or normal\n- Examples:\n  - **Parenchymal**: ILD (IPF, CTD-ILD, sarcoidosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis)\n  - **Pleural**: Pleural effusion, thickening, pneumothorax\n  - **Neuromuscular**: MND, myasthenia gravis, Guillain-Barré\n  - **Chest wall**: Kyphoscoliosis, ankylosing spondylitis, obesity\n\n**MIXED Pattern:**\n- **FEV₁/FVC <0.70** (obstructive component)\n- TLC **reduced** (restrictive component)\n- Example: COPD + pulmonary fibrosis (combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema - CPFE)\n\n**SMALL AIRWAYS Disease:**\n- Normal FEV₁, FVC, FEV₁/FVC\n- Reduced FEF₂₅₋₇₅ (mid-expiratory flow)\n- May have gas trapping (elevated RV/TLC ratio)\n- Example: Early asthma, bronchiolitis\n\n**This Patient's Results:**\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. **FEV₁/FVC = 0.93** (>0.70) ✅ **NOT obstructive**\n2. **FVC = 70% predicted** (<80%) ✅ **Reduced**\n3. **TLC = 65% predicted** (<80%) ✅ **Reduced - CONFIRMS RESTRICTION**\n4. FEV₁ and FVC **both reduced proportionally** (consistent with restriction)\n\n**Interpretation:** **Restrictive lung disease**\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (Normal)**: FVC and TLC are significantly reduced (<80%); symptomatic (dyspnoea)\n- **B (Obstructive)**: FEV₁/FVC 0.93 (>0.70) - rules out obstruction\n- **D (Mixed)**: FEV₁/FVC >0.70 - no obstructive component\n- **E (Small airways)**: Major abnormality is reduced TLC (restrictive), not isolated small airway problem\n\n**Severity of Restriction:**\n- Based on **TLC** (or FVC if TLC not available):\n  - Mild: 70-79% predicted\n  - Moderate: 60-69% predicted (this patient)\n  - Severe: 50-59% predicted\n  - Very severe: <50% predicted\n\n**Next Steps in Evaluation:**\n\n**For Restrictive Pattern:**\n1. **DLCO (diffusion capacity)** - helps differentiate:\n   - **Reduced DLCO**: Parenchymal disease (ILD) or pulmonary vascular disease\n   - **Normal DLCO**: Extrapulmonary restriction (neuromuscular, chest wall, obesity)\n2. **HRCT chest** - assess for ILD, identify pattern\n3. **Further tests** based on suspected cause:\n   - Neuromuscular: EMG, nerve conduction studies, MIP/MEP (respiratory muscle strength)\n   - ILD: Autoimmune serology, bronchoscopy ± lung biopsy\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- Spirometry: MBS Item 11506\n- Full PFTs (including lung volumes): MBS Item 11503\n- DLCO: MBS Item 11509\n- Interpretation by respiratory physician or specialist\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** TLC measurement is essential to confirm restrictive lung disease. Spirometry alone showing reduced FVC with normal FEV₁/FVC ratio suggests restriction but is NOT diagnostic - need lung volumes (TLC) to confirm. DLCO then helps differentiate parenchymal from extrapulmonary causes.",
