        "explanation": "This patient has severe impairment of diffusion capacity (DLCO 42% predicted), consistent with advanced interstitial lung disease affecting the alveolar-capillary interface.\n\n**DLCO (Diffusion Capacity of Lung for Carbon Monoxide):**\n\n**Definition:**\n- Measures ability of gas to transfer from alveoli to pulmonary capillary blood\n- Uses carbon monoxide (CO) as tracer gas\n- CO binds readily to haemoglobin\n- **DLCO = KCO × VA**\n  - KCO (transfer coefficient): DLCO per unit alveolar volume\n  - VA: Alveolar volume\n\n**Test Method:**\n1. Patient inhales gas mixture (0.3% CO, tracer gas, air)\n2. Holds breath 10 seconds\n3. Exhales\n4. CO uptake measured → DLCO calculated\n\n**Normal Values:**\n- Men: 25-30 mL/min/mmHg\n- Women: 20-25 mL/min/mmHg\n- Expressed as % predicted (adjusted for age, sex, height, ethnicity)\n\n**Severity Classification:**\n- **Normal**: >80% predicted\n- **Mild**: 60-79% predicted\n- **Moderate**: 40-59% predicted (this patient: 42%)\n- **Severe**: <40% predicted\n\n**Factors Affecting DLCO:**\n\n**Components of Diffusion:**\n1. **Alveolar-capillary membrane** (thickness, surface area)\n2. **Capillary blood volume**\n3. **Haemoglobin concentration** (CO binding sites)\n4. **Ventilation-perfusion matching**\n\n**Reduced DLCO Causes:**\n\n**Parenchymal Lung Disease:**\n- ✅ **Interstitial lung disease (ILD)** - MOST COMMON\n  - Thickened alveolar-capillary membrane\n  - Reduced surface area (fibrosis)\n  - Examples: IPF, CTD-ILD, sarcoidosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis\n- **Emphysema** (loss of alveolar surface area)\n\n**Pulmonary Vascular:**\n- Pulmonary hypertension (reduced capillary blood volume)\n- Pulmonary embolism\n- Vasculitis\n\n**Haematological:**\n- **Anaemia** (fewer Hb binding sites for CO)\n- Correction formula: DLCO_corrected = DLCO_measured × (1.7 × Hb / (Hb + 10.22))\n\n**Other:**\n- Pneumonectomy/lobectomy (reduced lung volume)\n- Alveolar haemorrhage (acute - CO binds to blood in alveoli)\n\n**Increased DLCO Causes:**\n- **Alveolar haemorrhage** (CO binds to RBCs in alveoli)\n- **Polycythaemia** (increased Hb)\n- **Asthma** (increased capillary blood volume)\n- **Left-to-right cardiac shunt**\n- **Supine position** (increased pulmonary blood volume)\n\n**This Patient's Results:**\n\n**Interpretation:**\n1. **Restrictive pattern**: FEV₁/FVC 0.86 (>0.70), TLC 62% (<80%)\n2. **Severely reduced DLCO**: 42% predicted\n3. **Known ILD**: Consistent with fibrotic lung disease\n4. **Severity**: Moderate restriction, severe diffusion impairment\n\n**Clinical Significance:**\n- Reduced DLCO indicates impaired gas exchange\n- Patient at risk of:\n  - **Exertional hypoxaemia** (may need ambulatory oxygen)\n  - **Pulmonary hypertension** (chronic hypoxia → vasoconstriction)\n  - **Exercise limitation**\n- DLCO often more sensitive than spirometry/TLC in early ILD\n- **DLCO decline over time** predicts mortality in IPF\n\n**Why Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n- **A (Normal gas exchange)**: DLCO 42% is severely abnormal, not normal (normal >80%)\n- **C (Vascular only)**: ILD is parenchymal disease; DLCO reduced due to thickened membrane + reduced surface area\n- **D (Measurement error)**: Result consistent with known ILD and restrictive pattern; no indication of error\n- **E (Anaemia)**: Possible contributor but unlikely sole cause of this degree of reduction; would expect less severe reduction from anaemia alone; question states \"known ILD\"\n\n**Clinical Use of DLCO in ILD:**\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Helps differentiate parenchymal (reduced DLCO) from extrapulmonary restriction (normal DLCO)\n\n**Monitoring:**\n- Serial DLCO tracks disease progression\n- Decline >10-15% over 6-12 months = significant worsening\n\n**Prognosis:**\n- DLCO <40% associated with poor prognosis in IPF\n- Strong predictor of mortality\n\n**Pre-operative Assessment:**\n- DLCO <40% increases surgical risk (thoracic surgery)\n\n**Oxygen Prescription:**\n- Reduced DLCO → assess for desaturation on exertion\n- May need ambulatory oxygen assessment\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- DLCO measurement: MBS Item 11509\n- Specialist interpretation required\n- Results guide treatment decisions in ILD\n- Low DLCO (<40%) may support antifibrotic therapy in IPF\n\n**Clinical Pearl:** In ILD, DLCO is often more sensitive than FVC for detecting early disease and predicting prognosis. DLCO <40% predicts worse outcomes and may trigger consideration of lung transplant referral. Always check haemoglobin and correct DLCO if anaemic.",
